; metacalc formula drawing for the TI-84 Plus SE. Holds the statements of one assertion of the
; library, lays out the selected one, unfolded, and draws it on the canvas pixel for pixel as the
; host model does (crates/mcui, crates/mcglyph): the tokens of the statement, each the glyph codes
; of its symbol from the library's glyph map, placed in lines by mcglyph's place and drawn with
; ui_text on the text row of its line, only on rows inside a window. It also builds the
; subexpression table of each statement, the nodes of the model's Expr, folds nodes behind an
; ellipsis and inverts a node's runs as a cursor. Fitting a statement to a number of lines is
; not here.
;
; Code runs from flash in bank A and is position dependent. An including file defines FM_EMBEDDED
; first and places the code itself, after the reader (lib/) and the canvas (ui/) it calls, with -I
; at the repository root and at app/src. The canvas's ui_set_font must have been called before
; fm_open, and the font stays the one set until the next fm_open: fm measures each symbol's token
; and the ellipsis, UI_FONT_CH_2026, once per open and fm_draw draws with the font in use.
;
; Memory, placed by app/src/ram_map.inc. FM_RAM is in bank C of the kernel's spare page
; KERNEL_RAM_PAGE, from KERNEL_RAM_HIGH, $1E00 bytes, which the kernel uses only inside a kernel
; call; fm maps the page in every public routine and keeps everything there:
;   +$000 $100  variables (fm/ram.inc)
;   +$100 $100  fm's private stack, FM_STACK_TOP at +$200
;   +$200 $800  FM_GLYPHS, the library's glyph map section as lib_glyphs finds it
;   +$A00 $800  FM_SECOND, the library's shapes section as lib_shapes finds it
;   +$1200 $C00 FM_TOKENS, the tokens of the loaded statements one after the other, at most 768,
;               a record of four bytes each: the symbol id, the width in pixels, the columns
;               between the token and the one before it, 1 or 3, and its spacing flags. Folds
;               rewrite the width, the gap and the flags of the selected statement's records
;               (fold.inc): a token a fold hides has width 0 and gap 0, the first token of a
;               folded span has the ellipsis width, its gap and flags 4 | 80h, bit 7 marking the
;               ellipsis. A hidden token's width and gap add up to 0, so ly_scan and ly_draw
;               always fit it on the line it follows, at any width and after a first token wider
;               than the width, and a line starts only where a token did not fit; token 0 is
;               never hidden, so the visible tokens are placed as the model places them and
;               fm_lay never reads the node table.
; FM_LOW is $1100 bytes from KERNEL_RAM_LOW + $100 in the same page, an overlay on the kernel's low
; region, and the kernel's first page, with its parameters, its save area and the ready mark, is
; never written but for kv_ready:
;   +$000 $100  FM_HDR, a header of eight bytes for each loaded statement, at most 32: the address
;               of its first token record, its token count, its node count (0 until fm_nodes has
;               built its table), the first row of its table in the node planes, its number for
;               lib_tree, and a byte that is 1 when a fold has changed since the records were
;               last rewritten
;   +$100 $100  FM_TLEN, FM_TCNT at +$200 and FM_TSIZE at +$300: what fm_nodes keeps for each node
;               of the stored tree, at most 255 (nodes.inc)
;   +$400 $200  FM_PILE, fm_nodes' explicit stack: 255 pairs of two bytes, one for each tree
;               node still open, so a tree of 255 nodes cannot overrun it; fm_open holds the
;               syntax axioms' arities there, a byte each, at most 383, while it checks the shapes
;   +$600 $100  FM_SYMW, the width of each symbol's token, valid where its flags byte is set
;   +$700 $100  FM_SYMF, the token's spacing flags with bit 7 set once its width is measured;
;               fm_open clears the entries of the library's symbols
;   +$800 $180  FM_FIRST, the first token of each node of the node tables, the tables of the
;               statements one after the other, at most 384 rows
;   +$980 $180  FM_LAST, the last token of each node
;   +$B00 $180  FM_DEPTH, the depth of each node
;   +$C80 $180  FM_FOLDS, the fold flag of each node, 0 or 1, in the same row as its FM_FIRST
;               entry; cleared for a statement when its table is built
;   +$E00 $300  unused, FM_LOW_FREE of it kept free
; FM_SCRATCH is 256 bytes in bank B, in saveSScreen after UI_VARS, for the buffers the reader's
; accessors write and the glyph codes of a run of tokens; it holds nothing between calls.
;
; Sessions. Every public routine runs as a session, as the kernel's calls do: it reads the
; interrupt state with ld a,i twice, turns interrupts off, reads port 5, maps the spare page, saves
; the old port 5 and SP in it, switches to FM_STACK and works there, and on every exit restores SP,
; port 5 and the interrupt state. Interrupts stay off for the whole session; the longest one
; measured is fm_open on the ring library, 484,449 T-states (81 ms at 6 MHz), then fm_nodes of its
; largest statement, 358,365, fm_assertion of its largest assertion, 187,619, and fm_draw of a full
; window, at most 180,804; fm_fold repeats fm_nodes with 3,300 more when the table is not built,
; fm_cursor alone takes up to 65,000. fm_rows, fm_draw and fm_cursor first run the pass that applies
; folds when the statement is stale, so the call after an fm_fold costs its figure plus the pass,
; up to 128,000 on the fixture and 50,000 on ring. The six-row frame of the ring statement with the
; cursor on node 0 takes 329,512 T-states today, over the 50 ms budget of 300,000: one ui_invert
; for each line is most of the cursor. The reader and the
; canvas run inside it: lib_read saves the caller's port 5, which is the spare page,
; and puts it back, with no stack instruction in between, and needs its buffer in bank B; the canvas
; touches only bank B, the stack and the font table it was given. A session calls no OS routine.
; Port 7 changes only inside lib_read, which puts it back.
;
; The kernel's contract. The ready mark kv_ready holds READY_MAGIC after a good kernel_init and
; nothing else makes the kernel treat it as set. On entry a session reads the mark: unless it holds
; FM_OWNED, some kernel entry (kernel_init, good or refused, or kernel_reset) has run since the last
; fm session and may have written either region, so fm is closed: every routine but fm_open then
; returns FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN and draws nothing. Then the session always writes FM_OWNED to the mark,
; so kernel_verify, kernel_crc_begin and kernel_crc_chunk refuse until kernel_init runs again. An
; fm call between kernel_crc_begin and the last kernel_crc_chunk therefore ends the CRC run: the
; next chunk is refused. A caller opens fm again with fm_open, which copies the glyph and shapes
; sections afresh; fm_open also keeps where the glyph section was, the flash page of the library,
; the constant, symbol and syntax axiom counts and where the shapes section was, and fm_statement
; and fm_nodes return FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN unless the reader's glyph section lies at the same offset,
; has the same length and is in the same flash page, and its three counts and its shapes section's
; offset and length are the same, or it has no shapes now and had none. A kernel_reset at the start
; of an app closes fm.
;
; The test image (crates/mcemu/tests/routines/reader_test.z80) starts at $2000, in page 0, because
; the kernel, the reader, the canvas and fm together are longer than one 16 KB page. The app
; (app/src/pages.inc) puts fm on the canvas's page and the reader on another, so fm's calls to the
; reader are far calls through far_lib_* stubs that destroy IX; fm holds nothing in IX across one.
;
; Calling. Status comes back in A, 0 meaning ok, with the zero flag set exactly when A is 0. Every
; routine destroys all registers but IY and keeps the reader's rule for ports 5 and 7 and for the
; interrupt state, which are the caller's again on every exit. The private stack is 256 bytes, FM_STACK
; to FM_STACK_TOP, and the fm variables lie right below it. The tests measure 14 bytes pushed, the
; deepest of any session they run; that is a measurement and not a bound, since the reader alone may
; push 16 bytes below its return address.
;
;   fm_open       in:  the reader open with lib_open
;                 out: A = status. Copies the whole glyph section into FM_GLYPHS and checks that
;                      every entry lies inside it, then copies the whole shapes section into
;                      FM_SECOND and checks it: the count is the reader's syntax count, the offset
;                      table fits, every item lies after the table and inside the copy and has 1
;                      to 255 parts, and each child of its axiom's arity appears once and no
;                      other does. A library with neither trees nor shapes opens, and every
;                      statement of it is one node. Marks fm open; closed until it returns 0.
;                      FM_ERR_CLOSED: the reader is not open. FM_ERR_NO_MAP: the library has no
;                      glyph map. FM_ERR_FONT: its map is for another font. FM_ERR_TOO_BIG: the
;                      glyph section or the shapes section is longer than 2,048 bytes.
;                      FM_ERR_BAD: an entry or an item they cannot hold, or only one of trees and
;                      shapes; an entry holds a glyph code of 0, which ends a string, no code at
;                      all, or more than 128. FM_ERR_READ: the reader refused a read.
;   fm_assertion in:  HL = assertion number
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of statements. Loads every statement of the
;                      assertion, the essential hypotheses in record order and then the
;                      conclusion, as fm_statement loads one: the tokens and for each its width,
;                      gap and flags. The conclusion is selected. At most 32 statements and 768
;                      tokens; a statement has at most 255. FM_ERR_LIMIT: more of either.
;                      FM_ERR_INDEX: no such assertion. FM_ERR_BAD: a symbol past the glyph map,
;                      or a token wider than 255 pixels. FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN: fm closed or another
;                      library open. A refused call leaves nothing loaded and nothing selected.
;                      The node tables of an earlier load are gone.
;   fm_select     in:  E = statement, numbered as fm_statement numbers them: the hypotheses from
;                      0, FFh the conclusion
;                 out: A = status, B = its token count. Makes the statement the one fm_rows,
;                      fm_draw, fm_nodes, fm_fold, fm_folded and fm_cursor work on. Reads no flash and copies no token or node:
;                      the stored statement is used where it is. FM_ERR_INDEX: the loaded
;                      assertion has no such statement, or nothing is loaded; the selection is
;                      kept. The statement of a store fm_statement loaded is number 0, or FFh
;                      when it is a conclusion.
;   fm_statement  in:  HL = assertion number, E = statement, numbered as lib_tree numbers them:
;                      the essential hypotheses in record order from 0, FFh the conclusion
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of tokens. The tokens are the statement's
;                      symbols as lib_hypothesis and lib_conclusion give them, the typecode
;                      first. Loads that one statement as a store of one and selects it.
;                      FM_ERR_INDEX: no such assertion or statement. FM_ERR_BAD: a symbol past
;                      the glyph map, or a token wider than 255 pixels. A refused call leaves no
;                      statement loaded.
;   fm_rows       in:  D = width in pixels, 0 to 255
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of lines the selected statement takes, with its
;                      folds applied. A token wider than the width sits alone on its line.
;   fm_draw       in:  HL = text row of the statement's first line, signed, -16384 to 16383;
;                      D = width in pixels; E = x of the statement's first column; B = the first
;                      text row of the window, C = one past its last
;                 out: A = status. Draws each line whose row is in the window, and nothing else,
;                      with one ui_text for each run of tokens one column apart: the codes of
;                      the run are put together in FM_SCRATCH, and a token three columns from
;                      the one before it starts the next run at the x after the last glyph plus
;                      three. The space glyph is two columns wide and would make the gap four.
;                      A run is also cut after FM_RUN_MAX codes and continues one column on.
;   fm_nodes      in:  nothing; the selected statement
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of nodes. Builds the node table of the selected
;                      statement, which is the model's Expr for it: the nodes in preorder, node 0
;                      the whole statement, the typecode token 0 of node 0 and of no other. Node
;                      n spans tokens FM_FIRST[n] to FM_LAST[n], at depth FM_DEPTH[n], from the
;                      first row of its table, which its header holds; the parent
;                      is the nearest earlier node one level up and is not stored. A tree node
;                      whose only part is a child has no node of its own: the child takes its
;                      place at its depth. A statement the library has no tree for is one node
;                      over every token, depth 0, as is every statement of a library with no
;                      trees; the host model, unlike fm, parses the grammar itself for a library
;                      that exports its database and stores no trees. A variable's symbol id in
;                      a tree is one byte, because fm_tree_node refuses a larger one. The table is
;                      built once and stays valid across fm_select, until the next fm_assertion,
;                      fm_statement or fm_open; a second call returns at once. A refused call
;                      leaves it not built, the statement selected and the other tables as they
;                      were. FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN: fm closed or another library open. FM_ERR_INDEX: no
;                      statement selected. FM_ERR_READ: the reader refused a read. FM_ERR_TREE:
;                      the tree ends early or holds bytes after its root, a variable that is not
;                      the token at its place, or tokens that do not add up to the statement's.
;                      FM_ERR_LIMIT: more than 255 tree nodes in the statement, which also bounds
;                      its table nodes, the depth and the stack at 255, or more than 384 nodes in
;                      the tables of the loaded statements together.
;   fm_fold       in:  E = node of the selected statement, D = 0 clear, 1 set, 2 toggle
;                 out: A = status, B = 1 when the flag changed, else 0. Checks D, then builds
;                      the node table when it is not built, with fm_nodes' statuses. A flag is
;                      cleared when its statement's table is built, kept across fm_select, and
;                      gone with the tables on fm_assertion, fm_statement and fm_open. A node that is not
;                      foldable, with no node after it in its table that is deeper, keeps its
;                      flag at 0 and returns status 0 and B = 0. The records are rewritten by
;                      the next fm_rows, fm_draw or fm_cursor, once for any number of changes.
;                      FM_ERR_BAD: D above 2, checked before anything is built.
;                      FM_ERR_INDEX: no node E in the table.
;   fm_folded     in:  E = node of the selected statement
;                 out: A = status, B = bit 0 its flag, bit 1 set when it is foldable, bit 2 set
;                      when it is hidden inside a folded node. FM_ERR_INDEX: the table is not
;                      built or has no node E.
;   fm_cursor     in:  A = node, HL, D, E, B and C as fm_draw takes them
;                 out: A = status. Inverts the node's runs, or those of its outermost folded
;                      ancestor when it is hidden, for the pieces it shows: on each line that
;                      holds some, the rectangle from the first one's x to the end of the last,
;                      one column wider each side and the text row's 8 scanlines high, only on
;                      rows inside the window and clipped to the screen. Call it after fm_draw
;                      with the same arguments. FM_ERR_INDEX: the table is not built or has no
;                      node A.
;                      The node waits on the caller's stack through the entry's interrupt reads.
; Other statuses: FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN, from every routine but fm_open.

#ifndef FM_EMBEDDED
.org $4000
#endif

#include "fm/ram.inc"
#include "fm/session.inc"
#include "fm/open.inc"
#include "fm/shapes.inc"
#include "fm/statement.inc"
#include "fm/store.inc"
#include "fm/layout.inc"
#include "fm/fold.inc"
#include "fm/cursor.inc"
#include "fm/nodes.inc"
#include "fm/tree.inc"
